{.... Notes by B.H. Wood. "The first round in the international tournament at
Bognor Regis this year was a quiet one; apart, only, from this one game, in
which an unknown eighteen-year-old from Gosport beat the experienced British
Championship contender, writer and broad­caster Gerald Abrahams." 
(Illustrated London News - Saturday 12 May 1962)} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. g3 Bg7
4. Bg2 O-O 5. e4 ({... The usual continuation, ...} 5. Nf3 {... is indicated
here - but Abrahams rarely steers the normal course.}) 5... d6 6. Ne2 e5 7. d5
{... It seems illogical to block the diagonal of his King's bishop in this way.
} Nbd7 8. O-O Nc5 $2 9. b4 {... Ouch!  Abrahams gets in the first blow. 
Brameld obviously expected White to have to protect his attacked e4 pawn by 9.
Qc2, or 9.Nc3 giving Black time to play 9...a5, so safeguarding the knight on
c5 against being dislodged by this very move, b4, at any rate for a
considerable time. After the game, Abrahams considered 9.b4 to be a mistake
owing to the resultant loosening of his queen's wing.  We would put the blame
on his rather colourless play over the next few moves.} Ncd7 ({... But if
Black now plays ...} 9... Ncxe4 $2 {... he would lose his knight by ...} 10. f3
{... for it has no haven of retreat.}) 10. a3 Nh5 {... Black prepares for the
customary ....f5.} ({.... intending to answer ...} 10... a5 {... with ...} 11.
Bb2) 11. Nbc3 f5 12. f3 {... with this, White may have planned to continue, if
given time, with Be3 followed by c5.} ({... White also had to consider, ...} 
12. exf5 gxf5 13. f4 {... but in this variation, Black's dark-squared bishop
would become a very powerful piece.}) 12... f4 {... Black prevents 13.Be3 and
has already envisaged the possibility of sacrificing his king's knight for two
pawns and a promising attack.} 13. g4 Nhf6 14. Bd2 h5 $1 15. h3 ({... On, ...} 
15. g5 Nh7 16. h4 {... Black had intended ...} Nxg5 $1 17. hxg5 Qxg5 {... with
good prospects, particularly since the White king is unable to escape to the
queen's side with Kf2 because of ...Qh4+.}) 15... hxg4 16. hxg4 Nb6 17. Qb3 {
[#]} (17. c5 Nc4 {... favours Black.}) 17... Nxg4 $5 {... It is somewhat
doubtful if this sacrifice stands up to a rigorous analysis but it is a bold
attempt and is justified by success.} 18. fxg4 $17 ({... Or, if ...} 18. c5 Qh4
19. fxg4 Bxg4 {... and if ...} 20. cxb6 $2 f3 {... with a winning attack for
Black.}) 18... Bxg4 19. Rf3 {... A surprising answer to the threat of 19....f3.
} ({... If, instead ...} 19. Bf3 Qg5 $1 {... is strong for Black.}) 19... Kf7 (
{... Abrahams hopes for ...} 19... Bxf3 20. Bxf3 Qg5+ 21. Kf1 Qh4 22. Ng1 {
... followed by Be1 and Ra2, when he is well defended and his minor pieces
come into active play.}) 20. Kf1 ({... White should certainly have interposed .
..} 20. c5 {... here.  As the game goes, the knight at b6 plays an important
part in the final attack.}) 20... Rh8 21. Ng1 ({... Again, ...} 21. c5 {
... was to be preferred.}) 21... Rh2 22. Bxf4 $5 {... The move played is an
ingenious attempt, ...} ({... This was his last chance to play ...} 22. c5) {
... There are, however, more weaknesses in White's game than, probabl;y,
either player realised.  As play goes, his bright 9.b4 tells against him for
it has deprived his QN of pawn support and after Black's next, it is pinned
against the QR.} 22... exf4 $1 {... In this way, Black keeps his attack alive.}
({... for if now ...} 22... Rxg2 $2 {... then ...} 23. Bg5+ {... etc. ...}) ({
... and on ...} 22... Bxf3 $6 23. Bxh2 {... White remains a safe piece ahead.})
23. Rxf4+ Kg8 24. Rxg4 Qf6+ {[#]} 25. Ke2 $2 ({... The best chance was ...} 25.
Nf3 {... in order to answer ...} Qxc3 $2 ({... Black, however, has a much
stronger method of continuing the attack after 25.Nf3, in ...} 25... Rf8 $1 {
... threatening 26.... Rxg2 and 27....Qf3+.} 26. e5 Qf5 27. Rg3 Bxe5 {... etc.
...}) {... with, ...} 26. Qxc3 Bxc3 27. Rc1 {...and White finishes at least
the exchange ahead.}) 25... Rf8 $1 {... There is no satisfactory reply to this,
...} (25... Qxc3 26. Qxc3 Bxc3 27. Rc1 {... would have been much weaker.}) 26.
Re1 (26. Nf3 Rxg2+ $1 {... etc. ...}) ({... or, ...} 26. Rf1 $4 Qxf1+ {...
Brameld has conducted the attack in excellent style.}) 26... Qf2+ 27. Kd1 Qd4+
28. Kc2 Nxc4 29. Nd1 $2 (29. Re2 {... offered more resistance.} {... Black
would then however, win with ...} Ne5 {... threatening 30....Nxg4 and 30....
Qxg1, ...} {... and if ...} 30. Nf3 Qd3+ {... is decisive.}) 29... Qd2+ 30. Kb1
Qxe1 {Sources: CHESS, Vol.27, no.408-9, June 1962, p251; Illustrated London
News - Saturday 12 May 1962} 0-1

