1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 {... 0-1, 24. CHESS,
June 1962, doesn't give any moves of the game but says it was a "Sicilian
Najdorf with 6 Be2," i.e. 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6
6. Be2... Deutsche Schachzeitung 1962, 233, gave a very different continuation
from move 17 onwards. However, it turns out they confused this game with
Abrahams-Brameld played in the same round. My thanks to Arthur Brameld and
Ulrich Tamm for info.} 0-1

