[ECO "A85"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "4nk2/pp1b2n1/3p2q1/3P1pp1/PP1P4/1B1N1PP1/4NK2/7Q w - - 0 37"]
[PlyCount "25"]
[EventDate "1962.04.25"]
[EventType "swiss"]
[EventRounds "11"]
[EventCountry "ENG"]

{1. d4 f5 2. c4 Nf6 3. Nc3 e6 4. e3... I was particularly struck by D. E.
Lloyd’s defence from this position against Dr. Heinz Lehmann, Berlin, who
finished runner-up. Lloyd had lost a pawn in the opening through a pure
oversight. The long back-to-the-wall struggle which followed made fearsome
demands on his clock time so that in this complex situation he was left with
barely three minutes for his next nine moves, whereas Lehmann had the best
part of half an hour. Yet Lloyd saw through complications for which we have
space only to hint here.} 37. b5 f4 {Attacking a white knight and preparing to
expose White’s king.} 38. Nb4 fxg3+ 39. Nxg3 Qf6 40. Nc2 g4 41. Ne4 Qf4 42.
Ne3 Bf5 43. Ng3 Qxd4 {He has recovered the pawn and threatens 44...Qb2+ and 45.
..Qxb3; or alternatively 44...Qd2+ 45 Ne2 g3+; but he has about five seconds
left now...} 44. Qh8+ Ke7 45. fxg4 ({... yet he has seen that} 45. Ngxf5+ Nxf5
{46 Nxf5+ cannot be played because one knight is pinned}) ({moreover that after
} 45. Ngxf5+ Nxf5 {his knight protects his queen.}) 45... Bxg4 46. Qh6 ({
Moreover, that} 46. Qh4+ {would not win the bishop now because of the reply}
Nf6) 46... Qf6+ 47. Qxf6+ Nxf6 48. Ke1 Bf3 49. Kd2 {and Lloyd gained a
well-earned draw. Source: Illustrated London News - Saturday 19 May 1962, p34.
Comments by BH Wood.} 1/2-1/2

