{A game which, unless somebody does some­thing about it, may go down as one
of the unsolved mysteries of chess history.} 1. Nf3 d5 2. c3 Nf6 3. Qc2 g6 4.
g3 Bf5 5. d3 Qc8 6. h4 Nbd7 7. Nd4 e6 8. Nd2 c5 9. Nxf5 gxf5 10. Nf3 Bd6 11. d4
Qc7 {White, who has played in the weirdest style, here— resigned! Mr.
Mazitis, genial R.A., accepted the resignation as a tribute to the concealed
threats he had built up, but the more he tried to demonstrate these, the less
convincing they seemed. Somebody suggested that White wanted to go off and
bathe, others that he had an important academic interview... Source:
Illustrated London News, Saturday 19 May 1962, p34. Comments by BH Wood.} 0-1

