1. Nf3 f5 ({... How dull the game can become after the customary ...} 1... Nf6
2. g3 g6 3. Bg2 Bg7 4. O-O O-O {... etc.  The Dutch Defence may be
strategically doubtful but as compensation , Black almost invariably obtains
lively tactical chances against the White king.}) 2. g3 ({... The gambit
variation, ...} 2. e4 $6 fxe4 3. Ng5 e5 $1 4. d3 e3 $1 5. Bxe3 Nc6 {... is
quite favourable for Black.}) 2... Nf6 3. Bg2 e6 4. O-O Be7 5. d4 O-O 6. c4 d6
{[#]} ({... The Stonewall Variation, characterised by 6....d5, is less dynamic.
Marovic-Lloyd, DE, from Round 10 continued (with slight transpositions of
moves), ...} 6... d5 7. b3 c6 8. Ba3 Bxa3 9. Nxa3 Nbd7 10. Qc2 Qe8 11. Qb2 Qh5
12. Nc2 Ne4 13. Nce1 $1 Ndf6 14. Nd3 g5 15. Nfe5 Nd7 16. f3 Nef6 17. Rae1 g4
18. e4 $1 {... and White convincingly exploited his superior development and
Black's weakness on the dark squares.}) 7. b3 (7. Nc3 Qe8 8. Re1 Qg6 9. e4 $1
fxe4 10. Nxe4 Nxe4 11. Rxe4 Nc6 $1 ({... Not, of course, ...} 11... Qxe4 12.
Nh4) {... and if now, ...} 12. Re2 ({... or, ...} 12. Qe2 {... same ...}) (12.
Re1 {... would be inferior because of ...} Nb4 13. a3 Nc2 14. Nh4 Bxh4 15. Be4
Nxe1 $1 16. Bxg6 Nf3+ 17. Kg2 hxg6 18. gxh4 Nxh4+ {... with at least a draw
for Black.}) {... then, ...} 12... Bf6 {... followed by ....e5, with
interesting and difficult play for both sides.}) 7... Qe8 8. Qc2 Qh5 9. Nbd2 a5
{... Black's great difficulty in this variation is the development of his
queen's side pieces.  The plan initiated with 9....a5, is objectively also
somewhat questionable, but it is at least an attempt, at the cost of a pawn,
to solve the problem of Black's queen's wing.} ({... If, for example, ...} 9...
Nc6 {... White constantly has the possibility of ...} 10. d5 {... It is
important for Black to be able to answer White's d5 with e5, which is not
possible when his knight is attacked.}) ({.... as an alternative, ...} 9...
Nbd7 {... would do nothing to solve the problem of developing Black's
light-squared bishop.}) 10. Bb2 Na6 11. a3 {... White prevents the possibility
of ....Nb4, and prepares an eventual b4.} Bd7 12. Bc3 Bc6 {[#]} 13. b4 {
... Ivan Nemet (he is of Hungarian origin), aged nineteen, youth champion of
Yugoslavia, demonstrated in his games at Bognor Regis that he likes to take
the initiative at an early stage.} ({... After, ...} 13. Bxa5 f4 {...
threatening 14.... Qxa5.} ({... Black could try, ...} 13... Ne4) ({... or, ...
} 13... Be4) ({... or even sacrifice a second pawn with ...} 13... Nc5 14. Bxc7
Nce4 {... In all of these cases, Black has some compensation for the
sacrificed material.}) 14. Bc3 fxg3 15. hxg3 ({... otherwise, ...} 15. fxg3 Ng4
{... would threaten ....Ne3.}) 15... Ng4 16. Rfe1 {... successfully defends
against Black's threat to capture twice on f3, e.g. ...} Bxf3 $2 17. Nxf3 Rxf3
$2 18. exf3 Qh2+ 19. Kf1 {... and White is safe.}) 13... Nb8 {... Now that his
QB is well placed, Black's QN can be satisfactorily developed on d7.  White ,
of course, was threatening to win a piece with 14.b5 Bxf3 15.Bxf3, etc.} 14. b5
({... Naturally enough, White is not impressed with his worthless extra pawn
after ...} 14. bxa5) 14... Be4 15. Nxe4 {... Now, Black obtains a strong pawn
wedge at his e4.} ({... But White was short of good alternarives....} 15. Qb2 {
... for example, would frighten nobady.}) 15... fxe4 16. Ne1 d5 17. f3 {
... This was the move that White has relied upon for it seems that Black's
pawn centre must now break up.} Nbd7 $1 {[#]} ({... Not ...} 17... e3 {...
since this pawn would inevitably be lost.}) ({... The best reply to ...} 17...
exf3 {... would be ...} 18. exf3 {... intending to answer ...} dxc4 {... with .
..} 19. Qe2 {... with a distinct advanatge for White.}) 18. fxe4 $2 {... This
loses practically by force.} ({... Black was threatening 18....Nb6, and White
did not fancy ...} 18. c5 {... because of ...} c6 {... when Black's centre
takes on a solid appearance.  However, White could then continue ...} 19. bxc6
bxc6 20. Rb1 {... with a satisfactory game.  This was doubtless the correct
continuation.}) 18... Ng4 19. h3 ({... Or, ...} 19. Rxf8+ Rxf8 20. h3 Nf2 $1
21. Kh2 ({... or, ...} 21. h4 Qg4 {... and 22....de4.}) 21... Nf6 {... with
the decisive threat of 22....N6-g4+.}) 19... Rxf1+ 20. Bxf1 {[#]} ({...
Clearly, not ...} 20. Kxf1 $4 Ne3+) 20... Ne3 21. Qd3 Nxf1 22. g4 {... WHite
has stored great faith in the fact that Black's knight on f1 is trapped.  The
rest of the game centres on White's efforts to win this piece.} ({... It would
have been hopeless for him to have played, ...} 22. Kxf1 {... because of ...}
Qxh3+ {... and Black, who can quickly bring all his pieces into action, wins
very easily.}) 22... Qf7 23. Ng2 ({... If, ...} 23. Nf3 $2 Ng3) ({... or, ...} 
23. Nc2 Rf8 24. Be1 Qf4 {... etc, .}) 23... Rf8 24. Be1 {... necessary to
prevent 24....Qf2+.} dxe4 25. Qxe4 Bd6 {... which threatens -  26....Bh2+ 27.
Kh1 Ng3+ 28.Bxg3 Bxg3 with a comfortable win - threat 29....Qf1+, etc.} 26. Qd3
{[#]} ({... On, ...} 26. Qxb7 Ng3 {... is decisive.}) 26... e5 {... Opening up
the centre.} (26... Nh2 $5 {... was tempting, e.g. ...} 27. Bxa5 $2 ({.... but
was rejected because of ...} 27. Bg3 Bxg3 28. Qxg3 Nf1 29. Qe1 {... etc.})
27... Qf1+ $1) 27. c5 Be7 (27... Nxc5 28. dxc5 Bxc5+ {... was unclear, e.g. ...
} 29. e3 (29. Kh1 {... is also possible.}) 29... Qf3 $4 30. Qc4+ {... and 31.
Qxc5.}) (27... e4 {... was quite a good alternative to the move played, ...} {
... but after, ...} 28. Qc3 {... the position would have remained rather
blocked.} ({... not ...} 28. Qxe4 Bh2+ {... etc.})) 28. e3 {... White permits
the decisive penetration of the Black queen.} ({... There was little to be
done against the threat of 28....ed4.  If, for example, ...} 28. c6 {... then,
...} bxc6 29. bxc6 Nb6 30. dxe5 Bc5+ {... followed by ....Nd5, with plain
sailing for Black.}) 28... Qf3 29. Bxa5 ({... A slightly better chance was ...
} 29. Ra2 {... when, however, Black wins with ...} Qxh3 30. Rf2 Nh2 $1 ({
... not, ...} 30... Ng3 $2 31. Rxf8+ Bxf8 32. Bxg3 Qxg3 33. Qc4+ {... and Qe6,
with adequate counter-play.}) 31. Rxf8+ Bxf8 32. Qc4+ Kh8 {... and, if ...} 33.
Qe6 ({... or if ...} 33. Qf7 Nxg4 34. Qxd7 Qh2+ 35. Kf1 Qh1+ 36. Ke2 Qxg2+)
33... Qxg4) 29... Qf2+ ({... obviously not ...} 29... Qxh3 $2 30. Rxf1) 30. Kh1
Ng3+ 31. Kh2 Ne2 {Black threatens 32....Qg3+ 33.Kh1 Qxh3#. Source: CHESS, Vol.
27, no.408-9, June 1962, p248} (31... Ne2 {... and, if ...} 32. Be1 ({... or ..
.} 32. e4 Rf3) 32... Qg1#) 0-1

