1. e4 d5 {... A favourite defence of Karaklaic's.  Of the four games at Bognor
where his opponent played 1.e4, Karaklaic adopted the Centre Counter three
times and won all of these games.  Against Hindle he defended a Ruy Lopez and
won that game also.} 2. exd5 Nf6 (2... Qxd5 3. Nc3 Qa5 4. d4 {... favours
White.}) 3. c4 {... As will be seen, this is not an attempt to hold on to his
extra pawn.} c6 4. d4 {... This is White's idea - the game transposes into the
Panov-Botvinnik Variation against the Caro-Kahn (usually reached by the moves,
1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.dc5 cd5 4.c4, etc.).} ({... Experience has shown that Black
has more than enough compensation for the pawn sacrificed after ...} 4. dxc6
Nxc6 {... etc., ...}) 4... cxd5 5. Nc3 e6 ({... The true Panov attack comes
after ...} 5... Nc6 6. Bg5 {... Nowadays, whether justifiable or not, Black
usually avoids 5....Nc6.}) 6. Nf3 Be7 (6... dxc4 {... leads to an inferior
variation of the Queen's Gambit Accepted (see CHESS No.396, Page 23).}) 7. cxd5
{... Botvinnik's favourite continuation.} (7. c5 {... was rendered inoffensive
in the game Ivkov-Germek, Bled 1961, after ...} O-O 8. Bd3 b6 9. b4 a5 $1) 7...
Nxd5 (7... exd5 8. Bb5+ Nc6 9. Ne5 {... also leads to difficult play for Black.
}) 8. Bd3 Nxc3 ({... For the main alternative, ...} 8... O-O {... see CHESS No.
396, Page 23.}) 9. bxc3 Qc7 10. O-O Nd7 (10... Qxc3 {... leaving Black
dangerously behind in development, was not to be seriously considered.}) 11.
Qc2 {... preventing 11....0-0? because of 12.Bxh7+} b6 12. Re1 Bb7 13. Ng5 Nf6
14. Bb5+ Bc6 15. Bf4 {... The point of his last few moves ...} Qd7 (15... Qxf4
$4 {... would, of course, allow ...} 16. Bxc6+) ({... And not, ...} 15... Bd6
$2 {... because of ...} 16. Nxe6 $1 fxe6 17. Rxe6+ {... etc. ...}) 16. Bd3 (16.
Bxc6 Qxc6 {... would greatly ease Black's defence.}) 16... Nd5 17. Bd2 Bxg5 {
... A difficult decision for Black - a valuable bishop is exchanged for a
menacing knight.} 18. Bxg5 Rc8 {... Black plans to castle, after due
preparation with ....h6, but first he wishes to gain a tempo for his queen's
side counter-play.  19....Ba4 is threatened, ...} 19. a4 ({... and ...} 19. c4
{... would be no answer because of ...} Nb4) 19... h6 20. Bd2 O-O 21. c4 Bb7 {
... An important finesse, clearing the file for his QR.} 22. Qc1 {...
threatening to win at once with Bxh6, ...} Ne7 {[#]} 23. d5 $5 {... Dr.Lehmann
is determined to sacrifice on h6!} (23. Bxh6 Qxd4 24. Qg5 Ng6 25. Bxg6 fxg6 (
25... gxh6 26. Bxf7+ Kxf7 27. Qxh6 {... gives White chances, ...}) 26. Qxg6
Rxf2 $1 27. Be3 Qf6 28. Qxf6 Rxg2+ $1 29. Kf1 gxf6 $19 {... with a winning
position.}) 23... Nxd5 $1 {... This also refutes White's plan of attack.} ({
... After, ...} 23... exd5 {... he plans ...} 24. Bxh6 {... though even then
it seems that after ...} dxc4 ({... not ...} 24... gxh6 25. Qxh6) 25. Qg5 Qd4 {
... the defence would hold.}) 24. Bxh6 Nf4 $1 25. Qxf4 Qxd3 26. Bxg7 $2 (26.
Qg5 {... had to be played.  The ending after, ...} Qg6 27. Qxg6 fxg6 {...
would have been better for Black, but White could have fought on.}) 26... Qg6 {
... Decisively threatening 27....Qxg2# and 27....Qxg7.} 27. f3 Qxg7 28. Re5 {
... White is still hopeful - he now threatens 29.Rg5.} Rxc4 $1 29. Qe3 ({
... For if, ...} 29. Qxc4 {... then, ...} Qxe5 {... wins easily.}) 29... Re4 $3
{... An elegant finish.} 30. fxe4 ({... On, ...} 30. Rxe4 {....Black wins with
either, ...} Qxa1+ ({... or ...} 30... Bxe4)) 30... Qxe5 {Source: CHESS, Vol.
27, no.408-9, June 1962, p250} 0-1

