1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 O-O ({... All comments by Owen Hindle,
published in "En Passant" March 2006.} 4... d6 5. f3 O-O 6. Be3 b6 7. Qd2 ({
... An important alternative is ...} 7. Bd3 {... Black then needs to be
careful for the thematic ...} c5 $2 {... is a blunder, ...} ({... After 7.Bd3,
Black needs to play either ...} 7... Bb7) ({... or, ...} 7... a6) {...
allowing ...} 8. e5 {... with the possibility of Be4 looming.}) 7... c5 8. d5 (
8. Nge2 Nc6 9. d5 Ne5 10. Ng3 Re8 11. Bh6 Bh8 12. h3 Ba6 13. b3 e6 14. dxe6
fxe6 15. O-O-O Nf7 16. Bf4 Nd7 $1 {... Revealing the power of Black's king's
bishop. [#]} 17. Bd3 Qf6 18. Nge2 Nde5 19. Bb1 Rad8 20. Qc2 Nc6 21. h4 Nd4 22.
Qd2 Nxe2+ 23. Nxe2 b5 24. g4 bxc4 25. g5 Qa1 {... In all three games, the
Black queen reached this square.} 26. h5 cxb3 27. hxg6 hxg6 28. axb3 Qa3+ 29.
Kc2 Qb2# {... Nicolai,W-Hindle,OM, Eastbourne Open 1966.}) 8... e6 9. O-O-O (9.
Bd3 exd5 10. cxd5 Nbd7 11. Nge2 ({... Here, ...} 11. Bh6 {... not always a
good plan anyway, ...} {... would be very doubtful in view of ...} Nxe4 {
... introducing the possibility of ....Qh4+.}) 11... Ne5 12. O-O {... White
plays safe renouncing the plan of queenside castling and g4 and h4, etc., ...}
({... Note that ...} 12. Bc2 {... would allow ...} Nc4) 12... Nfd7 13. Rad1
Nxd3 14. Qxd3 Ne5 15. Qc2 Bd7 16. a4 Qe7 17. b3 a6 18. h3 {... Now, Black has
to deal with the threat of 19....f4.} f5 19. f4 Nf7 20. Bc1 Rae8 21. Qd3 b5 $1
{[#] ... There is no king there to attack, but Black still has good play on
the queen's wing.} 22. axb5 fxe4 23. Qc4 axb5 24. Nxb5 Rb8 25. Na3 Rb4 26. Qc2
Rfb8 27. Ng3 e3 28. Nc4 Rxb3 29. Rd3 Rxd3 30. Qxd3 Bb5 31. Rf3 Qa7 {... Black
has netted a pawn, and now an effective queen manoeuvre finishes the game off.
0-1, de Groot-Hindle,OM, Eastbourne 1964.}) 9... exd5 10. cxd5 Re8 {... A
familiar idea, preventing the exchange of the dark-squared bishops.} 11. Bh6
Bh8 12. g4 a6 13. h4 Nbd7 14. h5 Ne5 15. hxg6 fxg6 16. Bg5 b5 {... White seems
to be making all the running, but now Black's queenside attack begins to take
shape.} 17. Qh2 Ra7 {... This 'second rank defence' is an essential resource
for Black in such positions.} 18. Qh4 Rf7 19. Be2 b4 20. Nb1 Qa5 21. Bxf6 Bxf6
22. g5 Bh8 23. f4 Qxa2 $1 {[#]} 24. fxe5 Bxe5 25. Rd2 Bf4 26. Nh3 Bxd2+ 27.
Nxd2 Qa1+ 28. Nb1 Rxe4 $1 {... The attack crashes through, ...} 29. Qxe4 Bf5
30. Qe8+ Rf8 31. Qe6+ Kg7 $1 {... Evidently, the bishop is worth more than the
queen here.} 32. Qe7+ Rf7 33. Qxf7+ Kxf7 34. Kd2 Qxb2+ $19 {... Rhodes,
HG-Hindle,OM, Eastbourne Open 1964.  0-1.}) 5. Bg5 c5 6. d5 d6 7. Qd2 a6 8. f3
{... Samisch has delayed his celebrated 'f3' which is normally played at moves
5 or 6.} Nbd7 9. Nh3 Ne5 10. Nf2 {[#]} b5 11. cxb5 axb5 12. Bxb5 Qb6 13. O-O
Ba6 14. Bxa6 Qxa6 15. Rab1 Nc4 16. Qe2 h6 17. Bc1 Nd7 18. Nfd1 Rfb8 19. Re1 {
... I can't remember what motivated us to agree a draw at such an early stage.
Black played a sort of Benko Gambit where queenside pressure is compensation
for the pawn sacrificed.  At other times, I played in many different ways
against the Samisch Variation but I had a special favourite method that
involved playing an early ...b6.  See the games / notes given at Black's 4th
move above ...} 1/2-1/2

